<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">East–West Schism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1054">1054</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Great Schism, Schism of 1054</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East–West Schism, also called the Great Schism or the Schism of 1054, is the break of communion between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>. It followed long disputes between the Greek East and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> West over the Filioque clause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, the bread of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> and the pope's claim to universal jurisdiction. In 1054 Cardinal Humbert of Silva Candida, legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a>, excommunicated Patriarch Michael I Cerularius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, who excommunicated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> in turn. The breach deepened with the sack of Constantinople in 1204 and has never healed, though Pope Paul VI and Patriarch Athenagoras I nullified the anathemas of 1054 in 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-west-schism.jpg" alt="East–West Schism" width="330" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barrikader · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Schism_with_former_borders_(1054).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The split between Western and Eastern Christians came from political, cultural and theological factors over centuries, and historians regard the mutual excommunications of 1054 as its terminal event. In 330 Emperor Constantine moved the imperial capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lost its status as the imperial capital. The First Council of Constantinople in 381 raised the see of Constantinople above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, which has been described as sowing the seed of the later rivalry.</p><p>In 732 Emperor Leo III the Isaurian, angered by Pope Gregory III's opposition to his iconoclast policy, transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> from the patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The West's rejection of the Quinisext Council of 692 led to pressure on it to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> customs such as fasting on Saturdays and using unleavened bread.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1053 Leo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> wrote a letter meant for all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> bishops, including the pope, attacking Western practices such as unleavened bread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>. According to J. B. Bury he did so at the instigation of Patriarch Michael I Cerularius, who himself closed all the Latin churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 1054 a legate sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a> travelled to the city to deny Cerularius the title of ecumenical patriarch and to insist that he recognise the pope as head of all the churches. The legation also sought help from Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, who had conquered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>When the legation's leader, Cardinal Humbert of Silva Candida, learned that Cerularius refused, he excommunicated him, and Cerularius excommunicated Humbert and the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a>. The legates' act is of doubtful validity, since Pope Leo had died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>There was no single event that marked the breakdown; the two churches slid into and out of schism over several centuries, with temporary reconciliations. It is estimated that in 1054 a slim majority of Christians worldwide were Eastern, and Timothy Ware observes that ordinary Christians in both East and West were largely unaware of the dispute. Scholars have proposed other dates for the schism, including 1009, 1204, 1277 and 1484.</p><p>At the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1272 Michael VIII pledged to reunite the churches. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Ferrara-Florence</a>, on 6 June 1439, all the Eastern bishops present but one, Mark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, signed an agreement accepting Western doctrines, but it bore no lasting fruit. The Eastern Catholic Churches consider themselves to have reconciled East and West by accepting the primacy of the Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> while keeping Eastern liturgical practice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East–West Schism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It debated the issues of the <b>East–West Schism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Abbot of Montecassino, he had been chancellor to Leo IX and a legate in the failed talks with Constantinople of 1054 that led to the <b>East–West Schism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians often date the <b>East–West Schism</b> from this act.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-16">16 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
